+# Opt-in authenticated xcli host bridge
+
+This transport is disabled unless the operator explicitly registers an endpoint,
+installation instance and server certificate fingerprint. The existing native
+`xcli_local` provider still refuses containers. Public providers continue to
+require public HTTPS and retain their existing DNS/SSRF checks.
+
+The relay runs natively on the host, listening only on a literal loopback IP.
+It uses mutual TLS and accepts only explicitly allowlisted client certificate
+fingerprints, even when other certificates were signed by the same client CA.
+The Model Gateway trusts one pinned, self-signed server leaf certificate with a
+matching SAN, not the public/system CA store. Certificate or instance rotation
+invalidates old frozen connections; create a new connection/profile version.
+Never mutate an existing frozen profile to make it usable again.
+
+Only the trusted Model Gateway holds the client private key and the independent
+xcli application Token. The API receives public registration metadata only.
+Sandbox Worker, Sandbox Runner and Publisher must receive none of these files,
+Tokens or bridge environment variables. This does not grant a sandbox access to
+the host, Docker socket or a model endpoint. Do not use host networking or a LAN
+listener to work around connectivity failures.
+
+## Private configuration
+
+Provision a separate Unix directory for each trust domain, owned by the process
+UID with mode `0700`; all configuration, certificate and key files must be regular
+files owned by that UID with mode `0600`. Symlinks and indirect filenames are
+rejected. The client bundle contains only `server.pem`, `client.pem`, `client.key`
+and `client.json`; never mount the host relay directory or client CA signing key
+into a container. Production certificate creation, trust enrollment and mounting
+require a separate reviewed deployment. Test certificates are not production
+credentials and expire after two days.
+
+Example host `relay.json` (replace every registration value):
+
+```json
+{
+  "version": 1,
+  "instance_id": "operator-installation-01",
+  "upstream_base_url": "http://127.0.0.1:18741/v1",
+  "listen_host": "127.0.0.1",
+  "listen_port": 18742,
+  "server_certificate": "server.pem",
+  "server_key": "server.key",
+  "client_ca": "client-ca.pem",
+  "allowed_client_fingerprints": ["<64 lowercase hex SHA256 of client certificate DER>"],
+  "allowed_models": ["xcli/text-default"],
+  "max_concurrent": 3,
+  "max_requests_per_minute": 60,
+  "timeout_seconds": 180
+}
+```
+
+Start the reviewed host relay with `contribos xcli-host-bridge --config
+/absolute/private/relay.json`. The command requires no GitHub, Publisher or
+application database configuration. Startup emits only public listener and
+certificate metadata; requests, bodies, Tokens and private file paths are not
+logged. The relay is a single-process service; its concurrency and rate budgets
+apply per process. No automatic startup/service installation is provided.
+
+The server certificate must be one self-signed leaf with `CA=false`, serverAuth,
+a current validity period and a SAN for `host.docker.internal` (and the literal
+loopback IP if native tests are needed). Use a distinct client CA and clientAuth
+leaf; pin its exact fingerprint in the relay. Do not reuse personal browser,
+native CLI login, SSH or provider account keys.
+
+Model Gateway `client.json`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "version": 1,
+  "endpoint": "https://host.docker.internal:18742/v1",
+  "instance_id": "operator-installation-01",
+  "server_sha256": "<64 lowercase hex SHA256 of server certificate DER>",
+  "server_certificate": "server.pem",
+  "client_certificate": "client.pem",
+  "client_key": "client.key"
+}
+```
+
+API and Model Gateway registration must agree exactly:
+
+```text
+CONTRIBOS_XCLI_BRIDGE_ENDPOINT=https://host.docker.internal:18742/v1
+CONTRIBOS_XCLI_BRIDGE_INSTANCE=operator-installation-01
+CONTRIBOS_XCLI_BRIDGE_SERVER_SHA256=<server certificate DER SHA256>
+```
+
+Only Model Gateway additionally receives
+`CONTRIBOS_XCLI_BRIDGE_CONFIG=/absolute/private/client.json`. In a container,
+provision the private directory for the Gateway's actual UID (normally 10001),
+not the host user's UID. The synthetic container probe deliberately runs under
+the temporary directory owner's UID; that is not a production permission recipe.
+Keep the private mount read-only. Do not relax directory/file permissions when
+UID mapping fails.
+
+The model-settings API exposes endpoint, instance and public fingerprint only.
+The settings UI offers `xcli_bridge` when it is registered. Save a separate xcli
+application Token through existing encrypted Gateway credential management.
+The connection freezes `bridge_instance` and `bridge_server_sha256`; profiles
+still use existing content hashes and short-lived scoped task authorizations.
+No SQL migration or rewrite of historical connection/profile records is needed.
+
+## Request limits and failure behavior
+
+- Exact routes: `GET /v1/models`, `POST /v1/chat/completions`, without queries.
+- One fixed HTTP loopback xcli upstream. No proxy environment, arbitrary URLs,
+  redirects, automatic retries or provider fallback.
+- Both mTLS and the independent xcli application Token are required. Browser
+  Origin requests and ambiguous/duplicate framing or authorization headers fail.
+- Explicit model allowlist; catalog results are filtered to it. Frozen model
+  capabilities are verified before sending prompt data. Streaming, embeddings
+  and images are outside this transport's supported operations.
+- Request body at most 4 MiB; catalog at most 4 MiB; completion at most 32 MiB.
+  Absolute request deadlines are at most 180 seconds. Concurrency is 1–8 and
+  requests/minute 1–60000, chosen by the operator. TLS handshake has a 5-second
+  deadline. These bound transport work; xcli task/model budgets remain required.
+- Upstream error bodies and redirects are discarded. Unavailable registration,
+  missing/expired/wrong certificates, certificate rotation, instance mismatch,
+  unsupported models and exhausted authorization all fail closed.
+
+## Acceptance and activation
+
+Run `tests/test_xcli_bridge.py` with temporary certificates and fake HTTP upstream.
+The xcli repository's `tests/model_gateway/host_bridge.py` additionally exercises
+this transport through a built xcli and real loopback mTLS, optionally from a
+resource-limited trusted probe container. No real model, provider key, native
+account login or business database is used.
+
+macOS/OrbStack success proves that environment only. Physical Linux amd64 Docker
+acceptance, Windows ACL support, production service supervision and real
+Plan/Review model acceptance remain open. Windows is rejected because private
+ACL verification is not implemented. Preserve the current Phase 5 physical
+acceptance gate in `docs/tasks.md`.
+
+Before activation, review exact image IDs/source inventory, endpoint/port,
+certificate fingerprints, installation ID, per-Gateway private mount/UID,
+model allowlist, budgets and a rollback to the currently repaired complete
+runtime. Obtain authorization for that concrete deployment. Do not reuse the
+old 200-file xcli integration rollout/rollback artifacts: ENV-R08 superseded
+that deployment by restoring all 202 baseline source files. Rollback removes
+bridge registration/mounts and leaves bridge profiles visibly unusable; it must
+not rewrite approvals, usage, historical profiles or business data.
